## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design tamper-evident, multi-tenant audit systems with cryptographic guarantees while meeting strict performance requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant cryptographic isolation and signing
- Append-only hash chains with Merkle trees
- Real-time verification with caching strategies
- Data locality and cross-region replication
- Retention policies with legal hold capabilities
- Performance budgeting under 200ms latency

## Code Example
```go
// Hash chain verification with caching
func verifyProvenance(tenantID string, fromSeq, toSeq uint64) (bool, error) {
    // Check cache first for sub-50ms latency
    if result := cache.Get(tenantID, fromSeq, toSeq); result != nil {
        return result.valid, nil
    }

    // Batch fetch from distributed log
    records, err := logStore.FetchRange(tenantID, fromSeq, toSeq)
    if err != nil {
        return false, err
    }

    // Verify hash chain sequentially
    prevHash := records[0].PrevHash
    for _, record := range records {
        computed := sha256(prevHash + record.Payload)
        if computed != record.Hash {
            return false, nil
        }
        prevHash = record.Hash
    }

    // Cache result for future queries
    cache.Set(tenantID, fromSeq, toSeq, true)
    return true, nil
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tenant key rotation without breaking existing hash chains?
- What's your strategy for proving correctness during partial network partitions?
- How do you optimize Merkle tree updates for high-throughput scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design SLA-aware routing with multi-tenant fairness, regulatory constraints, and cost.

## Key Concepts
- SLA/QoS, model pools, edge vs cloud, real-time routing, circuit breakers, fault isolation, observability.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for routing decision
function route(request, tenancy, pools, metrics) {
  const budget = tenancy.latencyBudget;
  const eta = estimateETA(request, pools, metrics);
  const chosen = (eta <= budget) ? selectPool(pools, tenancy) : escalateFallback(tenancy);
  return chosen;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test burst traffic and backpressure across tenants?
- How would you validate data locality guarantees under failover?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce cost controls and latency SLAs in multi-tenant LLM routing; requires architecture for dynamic model selection and burst handling.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant cost budgets and SLA targets
- Dynamic model selection to trade latency and cost
- Burst handling with backoff/queueing
- Observability and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
function routePrompt(tenant, metrics, prompts) {
  const budget = tenantBudget[tenant];
  const latencyTarget = tenantLatency[tenant];
  const candidate = selectModel(budget, latencyTarget, metrics);
  updateBudget(tenant, prompts.size, candidate.cost);
  return { model: candidate.name, estimatedLatency: candidate.latency };
}
```

